{"task_id": "HumanEval/49", "solution": "```python\ndef modp(n: int, p: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates 2^n modulo p using binary exponentiation.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The exponent (must be non-negative).\n        p (int): The modulus (must be positive).\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The result of (2^n) % p.\n             Returns 0 if p is 1.\n             Returns 1 if n is 0 and p > 1.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n < 0 or p <= 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Exponent 'n' must be non-negative.\")\n    if p <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Modulus 'p' must be positive.\")\n\n    # Handle special case where modulus is 1\n    if p == 1:\n        return 0\n\n    # Handle special case where exponent is 0\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1 % p  # Which is 1, since p > 1 here\n\n    result = 1\n    base = 2 % p  # Initialize base as 2, reduced by modulus\n    exponent = n\n\n    # Perform binary exponentiation\n    while exponent > 0:\n        # If the current bit of the exponent is 1 (i.e., exponent is odd)\n        if exponent % 2 == 1:\n            result = (result * base) % p\n        \n        # Square the base and take modulo p\n        base = (base * base) % p\n        \n        # Move to the next bit of the exponent\n        exponent //= 2\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/50", "solution": "```python\ndef decode_shift(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Decodes a string that was encoded using a 5-position backward shift in the alphabet,\n    wrapping around from 'a' to 'z'.\n\n    Args:\n        s (str): The encoded string, assumed to contain only lowercase English letters.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The decoded string.\n    \"\"\"\n    decoded_chars = []\n    for ch in s:\n        # Get 0-indexed position (0 for 'a', 25 for 'z')\n        pos = ord(ch) - ord(\"a\")\n        \n        # Shift back by 5 positions, handling wrap-around from 'a' to 'z'\n        # Python's % operator handles negative numbers correctly for our wrap-around logic.\n        # E.g., (0 - 5) % 26 = -5 % 26 = 21 (which corresponds to 'v')\n        decoded_pos = (pos - 5) % 26\n        \n        # Convert the new 0-indexed position back to a character\n        decoded_ch = chr(decoded_pos + ord(\"a\"))\n        decoded_chars.append(decoded_ch)\n        \n    return \"\".join(decoded_chars)\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/51", "solution": "```python\ndef remove_vowels(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Removes all vowel characters (case-insensitive) from a given string.\n    \n    Args:\n        text (str): The input string.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: A new string with vowels removed.\n    \"\"\"\n    vowels = {'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'}\n    result_chars = []\n    \n    for char in text:\n        if char.lower() not in vowels:\n            result_chars.append(char)\n            \n    return \"\".join(result_chars)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/52", "solution": "```python\ndef below_threshold(l: list, t: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Return True if all numbers in the list l are below threshold t.\n\n    This function iterates through the provided list 'l' and checks if each number\n    is strictly less than the given threshold 't'. It returns True if all numbers\n    meet this condition, and False otherwise. The function is optimized to\n    return False immediately upon finding the first number that does not\n    satisfy the condition (early exit).\n\n    Args:\n        l: A list of numbers (e.g., integers or floats).\n        t: An integer representing the threshold.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if all numbers in 'l' are strictly less than 't', False otherwise.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> below_threshold([1, 2, 4, 10], 100)\n        True\n        >>> below_threshold([1, 20, 4, 10], 5)\n        False\n        >>> below_threshold([], 5)\n        True\n        >>> below_threshold([5, 6], 5)\n        False\n    \"\"\"\n    # Using a generator expression with all() provides an efficient and Pythonic solution.\n    # It evaluates elements lazily and short-circuits (stops early) if any condition is False.\n    return all(num < t for num in l)\n```"}
